# Zod Request Body Validation

Validate every request body with a Zod schema in middleware (or the route layer) before the controller runs. Controllers must receive already-parsed, typed data — never raw `req.body`.

## Do / Don't

```typescript
// ❌ BAD — controller trusts unvalidated input
export async function createUser(req: Request, res: Response) {
  const { email, role } = req.body;
  await userService.create({ email, role });
}

// ✅ GOOD — Zod schema + middleware before controller
// src/schemas/user.ts
export const createUserSchema = z.object({
  email: z.string().email(),
  role: z.enum(["user", "admin"]),
});

// src/middleware/validate.ts
export function validateBody<T extends z.ZodType>(schema: T) {
  return (req: Request, res: Response, next: NextFunction) => {
    const result = schema.safeParse(req.body);
    if (!result.success) {
      return res.status(400).json({ error: "Invalid request body" });
    }
    req.body = result.data;
    next();
  };
}

// src/routes/users.ts
router.post("/", validateBody(createUserSchema), createUser);
```

## Checklist

- Define schemas in `src/schemas` (or equivalent); keep them out of controllers.
- Attach validation middleware on the route so invalid bodies never reach the controller.
- Prefer `safeParse` in middleware and return a stable 400 — do not leak internal Zod paths/stack traces beyond safe validation messages.
- Controllers use the parsed body (typed from the schema), not ad-hoc casts of `req.body`.
- Apply the same boundary validation to query/params when those drive business logic.
